NAME
    printf - write formatted text and escape sequences

SYNOPSIS
    printf FORMAT [ARG...]

DESCRIPTION
    Writes FORMAT without adding a newline. C-style escapes in FORMAT include
    \\, \a, \b, \e, \f, \n, \r, \t, \v, octal, hexadecimal, and Unicode
    escapes. \c stops output. %% writes %, %s writes a string, %b interprets
    escapes in a string, and %c writes the first character. %d and %i write a
    signed decimal integer; %u, %o, %x, and %X write unsigned decimal, octal,
    or hexadecimal integers. Integer arguments accept signs, 0-prefixed octal,
    0x-prefixed hexadecimal, and quoted character values. FORMAT repeats to
    consume extra conversion arguments.

    The flags #, -, +, space, and 0, numeric or * field widths, and numeric or
    * precisions are supported for string, character, and integer conversions.
    Width and precision are bounded at 65536 bytes. Floating-point conversions
    are not implemented.
